 ng\n\nPlease join the upcoming Slavic Colloquium event. Julia Verkholantse
 v (University of Pennsylvania) will present a talk titled "Etymology as Hi
 storical Knowledge in Medieval and Early Modern Historiography: Why Libuš
 e Married a Ploughman and Wanda Refused a Foreign King."\n\nFor premodern 
 historians\, names could contain knowledge about the past. The art of etym
 ological interpretation\, inherited from classical antiquity\, offered a m
 eans of uncovering that knowledge\, especially when it came to explaining 
 predestination and corroborating orally transmitted stories about origins\
 , genealogies\, and political order. Focusing on the legendary female rule
 rs Libuše in the Czech and Wanda in the Polish chronicle traditions\, thi
 s talk examines how interpretations of their names helped shape narratives
  about gender\, rulership\, marriage\, and legal and political authority. 
 By tracing how meanings uncovered through etymological analysis generated 
 narrative possibilities\, the talk presents historians’ recourse to etym
 ology not simply as linguistic wordplay\, but as a means of establishing h
 istorical truth and making sense of the past.
